Topics: input nd output formatting, introduction to github and git

Input:

Input is the data entered by the user during program execution using the input() function

Example:
name = input("Enter your name: ")
age = int(input("Enter your age: "))

print(name)
print(age)

Output:

Output is the information displayed to the user using the print() function

Example:
name = "Alice"
print("Hello", name)

Output Formatting:

Output formatting makes the displayed output more readable.

Using f-strings (Recommended):
name = "Alice"
age = 20

print(f"Name: {name}, Age: {age}")

Using format() Method:
name = "Alice"
age = 20

print("Name: {}, Age: {}".format(name, age))

Formatting Numbers:
pi = 3.14159265

print(f"{pi:.2f}")      # 3.14
print(f"{1000:,}")      # 1,000

Introduction to Git:

Git is a distributed version control system used to track changes in files and source code. It helps developers collaborate, maintain version history, and restore previous versions if needed.

Features of Git:

Tracks changes in files.
Allows multiple developers to work together.
Maintains version history.
Supports branching and merging.
Enables rollback to previous versions.

Common Git Commands:

Command  	Purpose

git init	Initialize a new Git repository.
git status	Show the current repository status.
git add <file>	Stage a file for commit.
git add .	Stage all changed files.
git commit -m "message"	Save staged changes with a message.
git log	View commit history.
git branch	List branches.
git checkout <branch>	Switch to another branch.
git merge <branch>	Merge another branch into the current branch.

3. Introduction to GitHub:

GitHub is a cloud-based platform that hosts Git repositories. It allows developers to store, share, and collaborate on projects online using Git.

Features of GitHub:

Online hosting for Git repositories.
Team collaboration.
Pull requests for code review.
Issue tracking.
Project management tools.
Backup and remote storage.

Difference Between Git and GitHub:

Git	GitHub:

A version control system.	A cloud platform for hosting Git repositories.
Works locally on your computer.	Works online over the internet.
Tracks code changes.	Provides collaboration and repository hosting.
Can be used without GitHub.	Uses Git to manage repositories.

Basic GitHub Workflow:

Create a GitHub account.
Create a new repository.

Clone the repository to your computer:

git clone <repository-url>
Make changes to the project.

Stage the changes:

git add .

Commit the changes:

git commit -m "Added new feature"

Push the changes to GitHub:

git push origin main